%S A397404 1,4,7,14,19,28,35,50,59,74,85,106,119,140,155,186,203,230,249,284,
%T A397404 305,338,361,406,431,470,497,546,575,620,651,714,747,798,833,896,933,
%U A397404 990,1029,1104,1145,1208,1251,1328,1373,1442,1489,1582,1631,1706,1757,1848
%N A397404 Number of inversions of the (2*n)-th Josephus permutation with step 2.
%C A397404 In the Josephus problem with step 2 (where every second remaining person is eliminated), the order of eliminating m persons is the m-th Josephus permutation J_m (row m of A321298). Sequence gives the number of inversions of J_(2*n).
%C A397404 Also the number of inversions of J_(2*n+1), since eliminating the first person leaves the same relative order as J_(2*n).
%C A397404 Also the sum of triangular numbers obtained by repeatedly halving n: a(n) = Sum_{k >= 0} A000217(floor(n/2^k)).
%C A397404 Partial sums of A129527.
%H A397404 Mosaab G. K. Abdelaleem, <a href="/A397404/b397404.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A397404 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Josephus_problem">Josephus problem</a>.
%F A397404 a(n) = a(floor(n/2)) + n*(n + 1)/2 with a(0) = 0.
%F A397404 a(n) = Sum_{k >= 0} floor(n/2^k) * (floor(n/2^k) + 1) / 2.
%F A397404 a(n) = Sum_{k >= 0} A000217(floor(n/2^k)).
%F A397404 a(n) = Sum_{i=1..n} A129527(i).
%e A397404 For n = 2, 2*n = 4. The permutation is J_4 = (2, 4, 3, 1) (row 4 of A321298).
%e A397404 The 4 pairs with i < j where J_4(i) > J_4(j) are (2, 1), (4, 3), (4, 1), and (3, 1).
%e A397404 Thus, J_4 has 4 inversions, so a(2) = 4.
%o A397404 (Python)
%o A397404 def A397404(n): return 1 if n == 1 else n * (n + 1) // 2 + A397404(n // 2)
%Y A397404 Cf. A000217, A129527, A321298.
%K A397404 nonn,easy
%O A397404 1,2
%A A397404 _Mosaab G. K. Abdelaleem_, Oct 12 2026
